[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $M_n = \max\{|B_t - B_{n-1}| : n-1 \le t \le n\}$ where $B_t$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstandard Brownian motion. We need to find $r > 0$ such that with probability [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mone, for all sufficiently large $n$, $M_n \le r\sqrt{\log n}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me think about the distribution of $M_n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the independent increments property of Brownian motion, $B_t - B_{n-1}$ for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$t \in [n-1, n]$ is a Brownian motion starting at 0, independent of $\mathcal{F}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m_{n-1}$. So $M_n$ are i.i.d. random variables, each distributed as $\max_{0 \le [0m[38;2;124;124;124ms \le 1} |B_s|$ where $B_s$ is standard Brownian motion.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $X = \max_{0 \le s \le 1} |B_s|$. Then $M_n$ are i.i.d. copies of $X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to find $r$ such that $P(X_n \le r\sqrt{\log n} \text{ for all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msufficiently large } n) = 1$ where $X_n$ are i.i.d. copies of $X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic extreme value theory / Borel-Cantelli problem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe tail of $X = \max_{0 \le s \le 1} |B_s|$: We know that $P(\max_{0 \le s \le [0m[38;2;124;124;124m1} B_s \ge a) = 2P(B_1 \ge a) = 2(1 - \Phi(a))$ by the reflection principle. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly for the minimum. So:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$P(X \ge a) = P(\max_{0 \le s \le 1} B_s \ge a \text{ or } \min_{0 \le s \le 1} [0m[38;2;124;124;124mB_s \le -a)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy inclusion-exclusion and reflection principle:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$P(\max_{0 \le s \le 1} B_s \ge a) = 2(1-\Phi(a))$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$P(\min_{0 \le s \le 1} B_s \le -a) = 2(1-\Phi(a))$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$P(\max \ge a \text{ and } \min \le -a) = $ small for large $a$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor large $a$, $P(X \ge a) \approx 4(1-\Phi(a)) \approx 4 \cdot \frac{1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{\sqrt{2\pi}} \frac{e^{-a^2/2}}{a}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely, $1 - \Phi(a) \sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \frac{e^{-a^2/2}}{a}$ as[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$a \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $P(X \ge a) \sim \frac{4}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \frac{e^{-a^2/2}}{a}$ as $a \to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, we need to find $r$ such that $P(X_n \le r\sqrt{\log n} \text{ eventually})[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy Borel-Cantelli:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $\sum_n P(X_n > r\sqrt{\log n}) < \infty$, then $P(X_n > r\sqrt{\log n} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{ i.o.}) = 0$, so $X_n \le r\sqrt{\log n}$ eventually a.s.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- If $\sum_n P(X_n > r\sqrt{\log n}) = \infty$ and the events are independent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(which they are, since $X_n$ are independent), then $P(X_n > r\sqrt{\log n} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{ i.o.}) = 1$, so $X_n > r\sqrt{\log n}$ infinitely often a.s.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the critical $r$ is where $\sum_n P(X > r\sqrt{\log n})$ transitions from [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvergent to divergent.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$P(X > r\sqrt{\log n}) \sim \frac{4}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \frac{e^{-r^2 \log n / 2}}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{r\sqrt{\log n}} = \frac{4}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \frac{n^{-r^2/2}}{r\sqrt{\log n}}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe sum $\sum_n \frac{n^{-r^2/2}}{\sqrt{\log n}}$ converges if and only if [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$r^2/2 > 1$, i.e., $r > \sqrt{2}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAt $r = \sqrt{2}$: $\sum_n \frac{n^{-1}}{\sqrt{\log n}} = \sum_n \frac{1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{n\sqrt{\log n}}$ which diverges (by integral test: $\int \frac{dx}{x\sqrt{\log [0m[38;2;124;124;124mx}} = 2\sqrt{\log x} \to \infty$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo:[0m
